Puppeteer is incredibly powerful for bug bounties because it lets you automate actions inside a real browser engine, bypassing traditional script blocks and revealing vulnerabilities that raw HTTP clients (like cURL) miss.
Here are the primary ways bug bounty hunters use Puppeteer, complete with quick automation concepts.
## 1. Mass XSS Validation (Dynamic Verification)
Instead of guessing if a payload fires, Puppeteer can detect the exact moment an alert() or print() dialog triggers in the DOM.

import puppeteer from 'puppeteer';
const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
  executablePath: '/usr/bin/chromium-browser',
  args: ['--no-sandbox']
});const page = await browser.newPage();
// Catch the XSS alert if it fires
page.on('dialog', async dialog => {
  console.log(`[!] XSS Triggered! Alert Message: ${dialog.message()}`);
  await dialog.dismiss();
  await browser.close();
});
// Test your payload target URLawait page.goto('https://target.com<script>alert(1)</script>', {
  waitUntil: 'networkidle2'
});
await browser.close();

