Combining Puppeteer and Frida allows you to attack both the web frontend and mobile/desktop application runtimes simultaneously.
Here are the top use cases for using both tools in a bug bounty workflow.
## 1. Replaying Web Traffic Into Mobile Runtimes
You can use Puppeteer to scrape valid API tokens, CSRF tokens, or session IDs from a web app dashboard, then immediately feed those fresh values into a Frida script that modifies a mobile app's memory on the fly to bypass authentication checks.
## 2. Bypassing Advanced Web & Mobile CAPTCHAs
When dealing with targets that monitor user behavior (like Cloudflare or Akamai), you can use Puppeteer to solve or bypass web-based authentication flows. Once authenticated, you can use Frida to inject those valid session hooks directly into the thick-client or mobile application binary.
## 3. Automated API Fuzzing via Mobile Hooking
If a mobile app uses complex request signing (like custom HMAC headers generated in native C/C++ code), you cannot easily fuzz it in Burp Suite. You can use Frida to hook the native cryptographic function, expose it as a local RPC (Remote Procedure Call) service, and use Puppeteer to drive a local web interface that feeds payloads directly into the mobile app's encryption engine.
## Implementation Blueprint
Here is how you structure a script that coordinates both Puppeteer and Frida together in Node.js:

import puppeteer from 'puppeteer';import frida from 'frida';
async function main() {
  // --- 1. PUPPETEER LOGIC: Extract dynamic session token ---
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
    executablePath: '/usr/bin/chromium-browser',
    args: ['--no-sandbox']
  });
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://target.com');
  
  // (Perform your automated login actions here...)
  
  // Extract a secret dynamic token from localStorage or cookies
  const sessionToken = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('api_token'));
  await browser.close();
  
  console.log(`[+] Captured Web Token: ${sessionToken}`);

  // --- 2. FRIDA LOGIC: Inject token into mobile/native process ---
  // Connect to the target process (e.g., a local emulator or app)
  const session = await frida.attach('target_process_name');
  
  // Inject your instrumentation code, passing the Puppeteer token into memory
  const script = await session.createScript(`
    // Frida script running inside the target binary
    const secretToken = "${sessionToken}";
    console.log("[Frida] Overriding internal auth header with: " + secretToken);
    
    // Hook target functions here to inject the token dynamically
  `);
  
  await script.load();
  console.log('[+] Frida script loaded successfully with web session context.');
}

main().catch(console.error);

